title: 'Context meter + auto-checkpoint designed (Arky): kernel-owned gauge, a kernel-authored checkpoint turn, checkpointed model switch'
summary: |-
  The founder asked for a "context remaining %" with auto-checkpoint for Paradigm repos (and plain compaction otherwise), and for a model switch that checkpoints and recovers so nothing is lost. Arky's design:
  - Measurement: context = the per-message usage (in+cacheRead+cacheWrite+out) of the last main-chain assistant message, NOT the result.usage totals (those sum all calls in a turn; they would overstate ~10x and are also why Ledger cost may overcount). Sub-agent messages (parent_tool_use_id) are excluded.
  - Window: from modelUsage.contextWindow (unprobed), else a model table (≈), else unknown.
  - Ownership: the kernel owns a session.context row, the threshold policy (default 0.80) and the action. The GUI shows a core gauge plus a Checkpoint button; Ledger keeps the history.
  - Checkpoint trigger: a constant, kernel-authored inject at an idle boundary asking the session to call paradigm_session checkpoint, with a ONE-TURN scoped allow for exactly that tool+op (needs Aegis). Confirmed by watching the tool call; once per context epoch. Recovery: a constant recover inject after compact_boundary; later a Paradigm SessionStart hook plus a `paradigm session recover --brief` CLI.
  - Model switch: drain + resume({model}), probe-gated, since resume across models is unproven.
  - Contract changes: session.context, session.checkpoint, session.compacted for Claude, the model-switch drain reason.
  Open founder decision: should the kernel also run /compact at idle right after the checkpoint?
symbols_touched:
  - '#context-meter'
  - $context-checkpoint
  - $model-switch
  - '#claude-code-adapter'
  - '#supervisor'
  - '#ledger-mod'
learnings:
  - result.usage in stream-json sums every API call in a turn; per-message usage is the only correct context measure.
